What each panel shows:

(a) thrust vs PWM — your 11 bench points and the degree-4 polynomial that sim uses. The dotted line is W/2 per propeller, so where the curve crosses it, two props equal body weight (~1570 µs). The dashed line is your real hover point, 1335 µs.

(b) power vs PWM — same, for electrical watts. Note it's steeper than thrust: doubling thrust costs far more than double the power.

(c) residuals — how far the fits sit from the measurements, as % of reading. Thrust is essentially exact (worst 0.089%); power is worse at the bottom end (−3.65% at 1100 µs) where absolute values are small.

(d) propeller efficiency, N/W — the only panel that makes an argument. Efficiency falls 66% from 1100 to 2000 µs. That is the physical reason a policy that modulates thrust beats one holding constant hover: every watt spent above the minimum buys progressively less thrust. Your entire energy claim rests on this curve, and right now the paper asserts it rather than showing it.

Does it deserve a place? Panel (d) yes. Panels (a)–(c) not as they stand — and here's the honest problem.

You fit 5 coefficients to 11 points and then report the residuals of that same fit. That's calibration error, not validation error. Any reviewer who notices will say "of course a quartic fits 11 points," and R4 asked specifically for "calibration and validation of the energy/power model." Right now I can only give you the calibration half.

Since the robot is next to you, two things turn this into real validation:

1. Six interleaved bench points — 30 minutes. Measure thrust and power at 1050, 1150, 1250, 1350, 1450, 1550 µs — the midpoints between your existing samples. Those are held-out: the fit has never seen them. Then panel (c) becomes prediction error on unseen data, which is what validation means. Add them to PWM2TE.csv in a split column and I'll update the script to plot fit vs held-out separately.

2. The stronger one, and it's nearly free — end-to-end energy validation. You already record accumulated ∫V·I dt from the flight controller (356 J for the RL trial, 571 J for Decouple 1 in the IROS submission). So for every trial you have both:

predicted energy: integrate the power model over the commanded PWM trace
measured energy: the FCU's ∫V·I dt
Plot predicted vs measured, one point per trial, against a y = x line. That validates the model on the actual task, at real operating points, including battery sag and ESC losses the bench never saw. It's a much better figure than a bench re-measurement, and it directly answers R4.

If they diverge, that's also publishable — it quantifies how much your bench model understates true consumption, which is exactly the kind of detail the review said was missing.

